+#### Limit exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all priced against, or backed by, the same underlying (quote) currency - for example several pairs quoted in the same stablecoin, or a handful of futures contracts margined in the same currency - a large adverse move in that one currency can hit several of your open trades at once, even though each trade looked fine in isolation.
+
+`max_underlying_currency_exposure` lets you cap the fraction of your tradable capital that may be committed, at any one time, to open trades sharing the same underlying currency. It can be set in the configuration, or [overridden per strategy](#parameters-in-the-strategy).
+
+For example, with `max_underlying_currency_exposure=0.3` (30%), if you already have 300 USDT tied up in open USDT-quoted trades out of 1000 USDT tradable capital, no further USDT-quoted trade will be opened - or, if possible, it will be sized down so that the combined stake committed to USDT-quoted trades stays at or below 300 USDT.
+
+This mirrors how other stake-sizing limits (such as the [minimum/maximum stake amount](#minimum-trade-stake)) are enforced: the bot will first try to reduce the stake to fit under the limit, and will only skip the trade entirely if the resulting stake would fall below the pair's minimum tradable amount. This behaves identically in live/dry-run and in backtesting, since both use the same sizing logic.
+
+This setting is disabled (no cap) by default.
+
 #### Amend last stake amount
 
 Assuming we have the tradable balance of 1000 USDT, `stake_amount=400`, and `max_open_trades=3`.
